      {
        "id": "routesintro",
        "title": "The two coverage routes",
        "text": "Original Medicare is Parts A and B through the federal program. Medicare Advantage is a private plan approved by Medicare that provides Parts A and B. Both routes provide Medicare coverage, with different rules and costs. We can add the other coverage pieces after this distinction is clear.",
        "summary": "You can get Parts A and B through the federal program or through a private Medicare Advantage plan.",
        "question": "What are the two ways to get Medicare?",
        "source": "https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare/get-more-coverage/your-coverage-options"
      },
      {
        "id": "basics",
        "title": "What Medicare is",
        "text": "Medicare is health insurance from the federal government. It helps pay for health care. It is mainly for people 65 or older who meet the program’s eligibility rules. Some younger people qualify because of a disability, End-Stage Renal Disease, or ALS. You can learn the coverage pieces one at a time.",
        "summary": "Medicare is federal health insurance. We’ll build the rest of the picture one piece at a time.",
        "question": "What is Medicare?",
        "source": "https://www.medicare.gov/basics/get-started-with-medicare"
      },
      {
        "id": "enrollment_intro",
        "title": "Getting started with enrollment timing",
        "question": "How does first Medicare enrollment timing work?",
        "text": "For most people first becoming eligible at 65, the Initial Enrollment Period is a 7-month window: the 3 months before the birthday month, that month, and the 3 months after. The date coverage begins also depends on when enrollment happens and which enrollment period applies. Current health insurance matters: Medicare.gov checks the kind of insurance, whether job-based coverage comes from current employment, and employer size before giving timing guidance. Do not turn this general explanation into a personal start date, deadline, eligibility decision or permission to delay Part B. A first-of-month birthday has special start-date rules.",
        "summary": "The sign-up window and coverage start are different; current insurance helps identify the relevant timing questions.",
